  "text": "Miriam grabbed a timbrel at the edge of a dry sea. Exodus fifteen. Israel has just walked through the sea on dry ground and watched the water close behind them. And Moses and the children of Israel sing first. That is the order in the text, and it matters, so let us keep it straight. Then verse twenty. And Miriam the prophetess, the sister of Aaron, took a timbrel in her hand, and all the women went out after her with timbrels and with dances. And Miriam answered them, sing ye to the Lord, for he hath triumphed gloriously, the horse and his rider hath he thrown into the sea. She answered them. She takes the song and she leads the women in it, with percussion and dancing, on the far shore of an impossible crossing. Two things Scripture calls her right there. Prophetess. And sister of Aaron. Exodus two does not name the sister who watched the basket in the reeds, though Numbers twenty six names Miriam among those siblings, and that is the connection most readers make. What is certain is this. When the water closed, she picked up an instrument.",
